d.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bc4e24-a07b-5170-8926-cd699cf0f86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414256} {:answer/id #uuid "4c242cb5-3b92-57ed-ab90-2cdee34992fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414266} {:answer/id #uuid "7424f06b-159b-5c56-8bb1-e4165486d93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414287} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab1a084-e3fa-517d-98bf-385373080007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414293} {:answer/id #uuid "eafe5b8c-fdac-5f2f-8c20-f4eb3dda569f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\cdot {3}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ab1a084-e3fa-517d-98bf-385373080007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414293} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8924976-ac1d-5805-ab82-ea80f8fb3b09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1686501-a304-5a7a-bf6f-4c7790651929" :question/math-academy-id "q-216550" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B=\\{18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\\}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f:A\\to B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB=\\{18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}\\,|\\,\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $18$ | $12$ | $8$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $⋯$ | $18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=18{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0679db-895a-5a46-9a05-9b44fb8f1e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414315} {:answer/id #uuid "b51f41ec-ff36-5e1c-ac65-318bd0b22be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414322} {:answer/id #uuid "d98081a8-2046-5b75-ac9e-78b3fc9d7683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id 